From e46277be00e56332c00efea7b10bae808d200d33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: atomaton-engineer <atomaton-engineer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 16:10:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] release: hand the release pull request to validation, not to
 CI

The first release through the merged workflow failed at the merge job: fifteen
minutes after #573 opened, its `statusCheckRollup` held no `atomaton-check`. The
job that opened the pull request had dispatched `atomaton-check.yml` through
`dispatchCi`, which starts the CI jobs and nothing else. A check derived from a
`workflow_dispatch` run is reported on the commit but does not count toward a
required status check -- only the mirrored check run `validate_pull_request.ts`
writes through the Checks API does (`validate_pull_request.ts:23-29`). Nothing
dispatched the validator, because the pull request was opened with GITHUB_TOKEN
and fired no event.

`dispatch_pr_validation.ts` is the bridge that does both: it starts CI, waits for
it, and writes the required context. The release pull request now goes through the
same path an agent's pull request does, so the merge job's wait for
`.name == "atomaton-check"` is a wait for a check run that is actually written --
named from the ruleset's own required contexts, which is `atomaton-check` here
(`.github/atomaton/rulesets/main.json`).

The person flag needs its value: `parseArgs` reads `asked-by-person` as a string,
so the bare `--asked-by-person` from the write-up would have aborted the step
before dispatching anything. Verified, and pinned by a machinery test that runs
the release's exact argv plus a contract assertion on the workflow itself. An
empty `--ask` is accepted, because the step that would require one -- the agent
dispatch -- only runs when the pull request names an `/<agent>`, and the release
body names none.

No version bump. `dispatch_ci.ts` stays for what it does; its header now records
what it does not do, so the next caller does not make the same choice.
